# Orion: design doc (contracts are named RandomnessHub)

An ownerless, reusable randomness protocol for Arc. Any contract can request a random number and receive it in a callback, or read the raw beacon value directly. Randomness comes from the public drand `evmnet` beacon, verified onchain. The Street Race game is the first planned consumer, but nothing here is game specific.

## 1. Metadata

- Status: Implemented on testnet-ready code, not deployed. Internal review done; independent audit still required before mainnet.
- Authors: you, with Arc Studio
- Target chains: Arc Testnet first (chain 5042002), Arc Mainnet later after an independent review
- Language and toolchain: Solidity 0.8.28, Foundry, Cancun EVM target (Arc runs an Osaka baseline)
- Milestone: M1 verifier + registry, M2 hub + consumer base, M3 relayer + SDK, M4 first consumer (Street Race v2)
- Review tracker:
  - [ ] Design review
  - [ ] Security review (internal)
  - [ ] Independent audit (required before mainnet)
  - [ ] Ops review (relayer, monitoring)

## 2. Action items (living)

Done in this round:

- Contracts written: `RandomnessHub.sol`, `IRandomnessHub.sol`, `RandomnessConsumer.sol`.
- Internal review: no material findings. Static analysis: two false positives (time arithmetic and a zero check).
- 66 tests pass, using 18 real drand evmnet rounds. Tampered, negated, wrong-round and invalid-point signatures are rejected.
- Public key order for the constructor is `[w1, w0, w3, w2]` where `w0..w3` are drand's published key words in order. The published order itself is rejected.
- Measured gas (testnet-style fork of the test environment, not yet on Arc): first fulfill of a round about 293,000, a later request on the same round about 40,000 to 85,000.
- Relayer script: `bun run relayer`.
- Deployed to Arc Testnet: hub `0x78375fd8b1c5bf34ee3b0db08ff45290219883d0`, demo consumer `0x8d9cbd29cceb31e76e05eb38bf928ae9fe7183a6`.
- Live test on Arc: 17 of 17 passed. Real gas: about 258,000 for the first fulfill of a round.
- Finding: fulfilling costs about 0.005 USDC in gas, above `MIN_REWARD` of 0.001 USDC. Raise `MIN_REWARD` to 0.01 USDC (1e16) in the next version, and tell consumers to attach at least that.
- v2 deployed to Arc Testnet at `0xe754e552fae5ba55726f0e97a196ee9e19e2f9d2` and passed 17 of 17 live tests: `MIN_REWARD` raised to 0.01 USDC. 66 of 66 unit tests still pass. Logic is unchanged.
- Still open: deploy v2, independent audit, second source.

Open decisions for you before code starts:

- Protocol name: Orion (chosen 2026-11-07). Contract names stay RandomnessHub. Still to do: check for name collisions with other projects called Orion before any public launch.
- Reward model: requester sets the fulfiller reward above a minimum (proposed) versus a fixed protocol price.
- Protocol fee: none (proposed) versus a small fee to a treasury. A fee needs an owner role, which this design avoids.
- Scope of v1: single beacon (drand evmnet, proposed) versus mixing a second source.

## 3. Goals and non-goals

Goals
- Unbiasable randomness: no requester, fulfiller, validator or deployer can choose or predict an outcome.
- Ownerless and immutable: no admin keys, no pause, no upgrade, no fee switch.
- Permissionless fulfillment: anyone can relay a beacon value and earn the reward.
- Reusable: pull-style registry for any app, plus a push-style callback hub with a ready-made consumer base contract.
- Native to Arc: rewards in native USDC.

Non-goals
- No private or encrypted randomness.
- No randomness faster than the beacon period (3 seconds) plus relay time.
- No protection against collusion of a threshold of drand operators (see section 16).
- No cross-chain delivery in v1.

## 4. Requirements

Functional
- F1. A consumer can request randomness, attaching a reward, and gets a callback with a 32-byte value.
- F2. Anyone can submit a drand round signature; the first valid submission is stored once per round.
- F3. Several requests on the same round get independent values.
- F4. If a callback fails, anyone can retry it later.
- F5. If a round never arrives, the requester can reclaim the reward after a timeout.

Security (testable assertions)
- S1. A round stored in the registry always has a signature that verifies against the immutable public key.
- S2. The round assigned to a request is always strictly later than the latest round that could already be public.
- S3. No function lets any address change the public key, period, genesis time or any fee.
- S4. A request's reward is paid at most once and only after a valid beacon exists, or refunded at most once after expiry.
- S5. A failing or malicious consumer callback cannot block fulfillment of other requests or steal funds.
- S6. Contract balance is always at least pending rewards plus unclaimed credits.

## 5. Terminology and actors

- Round: one drand output, produced every 3 seconds. Round n is the n-th signature after genesis.
- Beacon value: keccak256 of the round signature, the shared public randomness for that round.
- Request: a consumer's order for randomness tied to one future round.
- Fulfiller: anyone who relays a round signature and triggers the callback.

| Actor | Onchain/offchain | Trust | Can do |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Consumer contract | Onchain | Semi-trusted | Request randomness, receive callback, cancel after expiry |
| Fulfiller / relayer | Offchain bot | Untrusted | Submit signatures, trigger callbacks, claim rewards |
| drand League of Entropy | Offchain | Trusted as a threshold group | Produces signatures; a threshold could predict future values |
| Arc validators | Onchain | Semi-trusted | Order transactions, set bounded block timestamps |
| Deployer | Onchain, one time | No privileges after deploy | Deploys with fixed parameters |
| Observer | Anyone | Untrusted | Read the registry, verify rounds |

## 6. Language and runtime

- Solidity 0.8.x for checked arithmetic. Compiler locked by Foundry config.
- Needs the BN254 precompiles: ecAdd (0x06), ecMul (0x07), ecPairing (0x08). A call to 0x08 on Arc Testnet answered, so the precompile exists. Gas behavior must be measured in a fork test (see section 20).
- Base contracts: OpenZeppelin `ReentrancyGuard`. BLS math from `kevincharm/bls-bn254` (MIT), not hand-written.

## 7. Transaction and execution model

- Every external function is atomic. State is written before any external call (checks-effects-interactions) and every function that sends value or calls a consumer is `nonReentrant`.
- The consumer callback is the only call into untrusted code. It runs with a fixed gas cap, its return data is ignored (no copying of return data), and its failure is recorded instead of reverting the fulfillment.
- Native USDC on Arc has 18 decimals in `msg.value`. All rewards in this protocol are native units; the ERC-20 view (6 decimals) is not used.
- Arc specifics: sends to `address(0)` revert and blocklisted recipients revert, so all payouts use the credit fallback.

## 8. Chain standards and interfaces

- Consumer interface: `onRandomness(uint256 requestId, bytes32 randomness)`, callable only by the hub.
- No token standard. No ERC-1271 or Permit2 needed.
- EVM role mapping table: N/A, this is a new design and has no roles to port.

## 9. Architecture overview

Two layers so apps can use only what they need.

1. `DrandRegistry`: verifies and stores round signatures. Pull-style, usable by anyone.
2. `RandomnessHub`: request, reward and callback logic on top of the registry (same deployment in v1 to save gas; separable in code).

```mermaid
sequenceDiagram
    participant C as Consumer
    participant H as RandomnessHub
    participant D as drand relay (offchain)
    participant F as Fulfiller
    C->>H: requestRandomness(gasLimit) + reward
    H-->>C: requestId (round = future round)
    Note over H: Request emitted. Round not yet public.
    D-->>F: round signature published
    F->>H: fulfill(requestId, signature)
    H->>H: verify BLS, store beacon[round]
    H->>F: pay reward (or credit)
    H->>C: onRandomness(requestId, randomness)
```

Flow of funds

| Step | Who moves what | Invariant |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Request | Consumer sends reward to hub | Hub balance rises by exactly the reward |
| Fulfill | Hub pays reward to fulfiller once | Request leaves Pending before payout |
| Cancel after expiry | Hub refunds reward to consumer once | Only if never fulfilled |
| Failed payout | Hub credits recipient | Credit equals the failed amount |
| Claim | Hub pays credit to caller | Credit zeroed before transfer |

Resting-state invariant: hub balance equals pending rewards plus unclaimed credits. The hub never holds other funds.

Dependencies and assumptions: drand evmnet keeps publishing and its key does not rotate; BN254 precompiles behave as on Ethereum; block timestamps on Arc are monotonic and within a few seconds of real time.

## 10. Contract design

Roles: none. There is no owner, admin, pauser or rescuer. Consumers and fulfillers are ordinary addresses.

Storage

| Variable | Type | Notes |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `PUBLIC_KEY_0..3` | immutable uint256 | drand G2 key as four words |
| `GENESIS_TIME`, `PERIOD` | immutable uint256 | 1727521075 and 3, to be re-checked against the drand info endpoint at deploy |
| `MIN_DELAY` | constant | 3 periods, safety margin before a request's round |
| `EXPIRY` | constant | 1 day after the round time before cancel is allowed |
| `MIN_REWARD` | constant | 1e16 native units (0.01 USDC), set from measured gas: about 258,000 gas, roughly 0.005 USDC on Arc |
| `MAX_CALLBACK_GAS` | constant | 500,000 |
| `beacon` | mapping(uint64 => bytes32) | stored once per verified round |
| `requests` | mapping(uint256 => Request) | see types |
| `nextRequestId` | uint256 | starts at 1 |
| `credits` | mapping(address => uint256) | failed payouts |

Types
- `Request { address consumer; uint64 round; uint32 callbackGas; uint128 reward; Status status; }`
- `Status { None, Pending, Fulfilled, CallbackFailed, Cancelled }`

Write functions

| Function | Caller | State mutated | Events | Reverts |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `requestRandomness(uint32 callbackGas)` payable | Any contract | New request, round chosen by hub | `Requested` | reward below minimum, gas above cap |
| `fulfill(uint256 id, uint256[2] sig)` | Anyone | Verifies and stores beacon if new, marks request, pays reward, calls consumer | `BeaconStored`, `Fulfilled`, `CallbackFailed` | request not Pending, round not yet public, invalid signature when beacon unset, too little gas supplied |
| `submitBeacon(uint64 round, uint256[2] sig)` | Anyone | Stores beacon only | `BeaconStored` | round already stored, invalid signature |
| `retryCallback(uint256 id)` | Anyone | Re-calls consumer, marks Fulfilled on success | `Fulfilled` | not CallbackFailed |
| `cancel(uint256 id)` | Request's consumer | Marks Cancelled, refunds reward | `Cancelled` | not Pending, before expiry, beacon exists |
| `claim()` | Anyone with credit | Zeros credit, pays | `Claimed` | nothing to claim |

Pseudocode: round selection and randomness
- `round = ceil((block.timestamp + MIN_DELAY - GENESIS_TIME) / PERIOD)`
- `randomness = keccak256(abi.encode(beacon[round], address(this), block.chainid, requestId))`

Pseudocode: callback
- Require `gasleft() >= callbackGas * 64 / 63 + overhead` so a fulfiller cannot under-gas a callback and force a false failure.
- Call the consumer with a gas cap, no return data copied. On failure set status CallbackFailed.

Events: `Requested(id, consumer, round, reward)`, `BeaconStored(round, value)`, `Fulfilled(id, fulfiller)`, `CallbackFailed(id)`, `Cancelled(id)`, `Claimed(account, amount)`. All ids and accounts indexed.

Read functions: `roundAt(timestamp)`, `getRequest(id)`, `beacon(round)`, `randomnessOf(id)`.

## 11. Specification tables

Feature: request and fulfillment

Principles
- A request's round is fixed at request time and cannot be changed.
- A beacon value is stored once and never overwritten.
- One request, one payout.

| Functionality | Restriction | Note | Event | Chain-specific adjustment | Audit note |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Request | Anyone with reward at least minimum | Hub picks the round | `Requested` | Reward in 18-decimal native USDC | Check timestamp margin |
| Fulfill | Anyone | Verifies BLS if beacon new | `Fulfilled` | | Check pairing gas |
| Cancel | Consumer, after expiry | Only if no beacon | `Cancelled` | Refund uses credit fallback | |

## 12. Deployment and initialization

- Constructor only. Parameters: public key words, genesis time, period. Constructor validates the key is a valid G2 point.
- Deterministic address (CREATE2) so the same address can be used for each network later; salt is a fixed string for the hub version.
- Genesis state: no roles, no balance. Deployment is a testnet deploy first, followed by relayer start.

## 13. Upgradeability

None. The contracts are immutable. Migration story: contract switch. A new version gets a new address, consumers re-point, and old requests drain through the old hub. Reason: any upgrade key would reintroduce the trust this protocol removes.

## 14. Key management and signing

- No privileged keys. Fulfillers use their own wallets; the reference relayer holds only a gas-and-reward wallet with no powers.
- The BLS signature is not an authorization for a user action, so replay protection is by round number and the immutable key. Randomness is bound to hub address, chain id and request id.

## 15. Security considerations

| Vulnerability | Applicable? | Mitigation |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Reentrancy | Yes (payout, callback) | CEI ordering and `nonReentrant` |
| Access control | Minimal | No privileged functions; `cancel` only for the request's consumer |
| Integer overflow | Checked | No `unchecked` planned except audited library code |
| Unchecked external call | Yes | Callback result handled; payouts fall back to credits |
| Fee-on-transfer tokens | No | No ERC-20 used |
| Signature replay | Yes | One beacon per round, stored once; key is immutable |
| Front-running / MEV | Yes | Fulfiller race only affects who earns the reward, not the outcome |
| Oracle manipulation | Yes (drand) | Single immutable key; see trust model |
| Denial of service | Yes | Capped callback gas, no loops over users, pull credits |
| Return data bomb | Yes | Return data not copied |
| Delegatecall / proxy | No | Not upgradeable |
| Timestamp dependence | Yes | Margin of 3 periods; timestamp used only to pick a future round |
| Approval persistence | N/A | No approvals |
| Centralization | Low | No admin; depends on drand operators |
| Consumer misuse | Yes | Consumer rules below |
| Pairing or hash-to-point bug | Yes | Use reviewed library, real test vectors, independent audit |

Consumer rules (documented in the base contract)
1. Freeze every input that affects the outcome before calling `requestRandomness`. The round's value becomes public on drand before it lands onchain, so anything still changeable in that gap can be gamed.
2. Do not let the callback revert on bad state. Record the value and let a separate settle step use it.
3. Have your own timeout, because a round can be delayed.

## 16. Trust model and threat analysis

| Privileged actor | Max damage if compromised | Mitigation | Detection |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| drand threshold group | Could learn or choose future values, biasing every consumer | None onchain in v1. Optional later: mix a second source | Monitor drand reports; compare to independent relays |
| Arc validator | Skew block timestamp by a few seconds | 3-period margin | Alert if `Requested` rounds look too near |
| Fulfiller | Delay delivery, pick who earns the reward | Anyone else can fulfill; expiry refund | Alert on requests pending longer than 1 minute |
| Consumer | Hurt only itself | Gas cap and isolation | n/a |
| Deployer | None after deploy | Immutable | n/a |

## 17. Emergency response

- No pause or rescue by design. A bug cannot be patched in place; the response is to deploy a new version and tell consumers to re-point.
- Consumers should expose their own pause or timeout.
- Stuck funds: a reward on a request whose round never arrives can be reclaimed by the consumer after expiry.

## 18. Failure scenarios

- Callback reverts: status CallbackFailed, reward still paid, anyone can retry. Other requests unaffected.
- Payout recipient blocked or reverts: reward credited and claimable later.
- drand outage: requests stay Pending, consumers use timeouts, rewards refundable after expiry.
- drand chain retired or key rotated: no new beacons verify. Existing stored rounds stay valid. Deploy a new hub.
- Fulfiller under-gases: reverts before changing state because of the gas check.

## 19. Priorities and tradeoffs

| Decision | Tradeoff | Rationale |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Immutable, no owner | Cannot patch bugs | Removes key risk, easier to trust |
| Requester-set reward | Requester may underpay and wait | Market pricing without an owner setting a price |
| Hub picks the round | Less flexibility | Prevents choosing an already public round |
| No protocol fee | No revenue | Avoids a treasury and an owner |
| Single beacon | Depends on drand | Simple, well studied |
| Callback plus pull registry | Slightly larger surface | Serves both integration styles |
| Rejected: block hash or prevrandao | n/a | Producer influence; prevrandao is 0 on Arc |
| Rejected: operator-signed randomness | n/a | Operator could cheat |

Prior art to study before building: Anyrand (same scheme, live on other chains), randa-mu `bls-solidity` registry demo, and D20DAO on Arc. This design differs by being ownerless and Arc native, but should reuse their proven BLS code and test vectors rather than invent crypto.

## 20. Testing strategy

- Branch coverage target: 95 percent of branches on hub and registry.
- Real vectors: at least 20 genuine evmnet rounds from the drand API plus tampered signatures that must fail.
- Fuzz: `roundAt` is monotonic and always later than the request time plus margin; one payout per request.
- Invariants: balance equals pending rewards plus credits; beacon never overwritten; status transitions only move forward.
- Hostile consumer tests: reverting, gas-burning, reentering and return-data-bomb callbacks.
- Fork test on Arc Testnet: measure fulfillment gas, confirm the precompiles.
- Static analysis: Slither.
- Gate before mainnet: independent third-party audit. Library crypto code is described by its authors as unaudited.
- Command: `forge test` and `slither contracts/RandomnessHub.sol`.

## 21. Third-party libraries

| Library | Version | Already a dependency | Why | Reviewed |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| OpenZeppelin contracts | 5.x installed | Yes | ReentrancyGuard | Widely audited |
| `@kevincharm/bls-bn254` | pin at build time | No | BN254 BLS and hash-to-point | Not formally audited, needs review |

## 22. Monitoring and alerting

| Event or metric | Threshold | Severity |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Requests pending beyond 60 seconds | any | Warning |
| Latest stored round lagging drand head | over 5 minutes | High |
| `CallbackFailed` rate | over 5 percent | Medium |
| Hub balance below pending plus credits | any | Critical |

## 23. Stakeholders

- Product owner (you): scope, name, reward model.
- Security reviewer: crypto library use and threat model.
- Ops: relayer wallet funding and uptime.
- First consumer team: Street Race v2.

## 24. Message encoding

N/A. No cross-chain messaging in v1. The only encoded value is the 64-byte G1 signature, passed as two 256-bit words.

## 25. Worked example

Street Race v2 settles a race.
1. Grid locks. The game stores the final racer list and calls `requestRandomness(200000)` with a reward.
2. Hub assigns round N, about 9 to 12 seconds ahead.
3. When drand publishes round N, a relayer calls `fulfill`. The hub verifies the signature, pays the relayer and calls `onRandomness`.
4. The game picks `racers[randomness % racers.length]` and pays out the pot.

Outcome: nobody on the grid, no validator and no relayer could choose the winner.
