# Orion: advantages and areas of application

All statements refer to the state on 7 November 2026. Comparisons with other protocols are based on their public descriptions; I did not read their contracts.

## 1. Advantages

### 1.1. No owner and no management keys
- The contract has no administrator, no pause, no upgrades and no fee. Nobody can change prices, swap the verification key or stop it.
- A consumer does not have to trust the protocol team. Reading the code is enough.
- For comparison: at D20DAO on Arc the owner, by their own description, sets prices and manages the source registry.

### 1.2. The result cannot be chosen
- The Hub itself picks the round for a request, always in the future (at least 9 seconds ahead). At that moment nobody knows the round value.
- The signature for a round is unique, so options cannot be tried until a convenient one is found.
- The consumer, the relayer and an Arc validator cannot choose the outcome. A validator can shift the block time by a couple of seconds, but the 9-second margin covers that.
- A block hash gives no such protection: its producer influences the result.

### 1.3. Verification right in the contract
- The drand signature is checked with mathematics (a pairing on the BN254 curve) inside the Hub. There is no need to trust the relayer, it can only deliver the signature.
- Anyone can check any round value and recompute a request's number from public data.

### 1.4. Open execution
- The reward goes to whoever first brings a correct signature. No permission, registration or operator list is needed.
- If one bot is silent, another takes its place. This improves availability.

### 1.5. Payments in USDC
- The reward is paid in native USDC, which on Arc is also gas. No separate token, subscription or prepayment is needed.
- The requester sets the reward: the market determines the price, as long as it is above the minimum (0.01 USDC).

### 1.6. Protection of the consumer from mistakes
- A failing or hostile consumer cannot block fulfillment for others or steal money.
- If the consumer's call failed, the value is stored and delivery can be retried.
- If a payout failed (the recipient is blocked), the amount is recorded as a credit.
- If the round never arrived, the reward can be taken back after a day.

### 1.7. One round serves many requests
- A round value is verified and stored once. Later requests for the same round need no check.
- The round registry can be read without a callback if only a timestamp and a number are needed.

### 1.8. Checked on live data
- 66 unit tests on 18 real drand rounds and 17 checks on the live Arc Testnet network.
- Two independent security reviews with no material findings. An independent audit is still needed.

### 1.9. A base for extension
- The architecture allows adding a second source (another beacon or an external service), and then both would have to be broken.

## 2. Honest limitations

| Limitation | What it means |
| --- | --- |
| The answer is not instant | About 10 seconds until the round is published, plus delivery time. Not suitable for every scenario |
| Cost of one request | A reward from 0.01 USDC plus the requester's gas. Expensive for frequent small rolls |
| Trust in the drand group | If a threshold of its operators collude, they could learn values in advance |
| Dependence on the evmnet network | If it is shut down, a new Hub version is needed |
| The verification library has no formal audit | An independent audit is needed before real money |
| Cannot be fixed in place | Any change means a new version and moving consumers |
| Rules for consumers | Inputs must be frozen before the request and a timeout of your own is needed |
| Gas price | If gas on Arc rises a lot, the minimum reward may stop covering the relayer |

## 3. Areas of application

### 3.1. A good fit

| Area | Why randomness is needed |
| --- | --- |
| Raffles and lotteries in USDC | A fair choice of a winner from a list of participants |
| Prize savings | Deposits where the interest forms a prize for a random depositor |
| NFT and collection launches | Random traits and a fair reveal of rarity |
| Allocation of limited spots | A random pick from applications when distributing tokens, tickets, access |
| DAOs and governance | Random selection of jurors, reviewers, committee members so they cannot be bribed in advance |
| Auctions and disputes | Breaking ties, a random arbiter in escrow |
| Choosing executors | A random pick of a keeper, operator or agent from a list |
| Spot checks | A random sample of payments or applications for review |
| Games and wagers with rare large outcomes | One number per round of the game, not per roll |

### 3.2. A side feature: a time lock
A round value cannot be known before its time. This can be used for sealed bids, delayed reveals and schedules, even when randomness itself is not needed.

### 3.3. A poor fit
- Instant results in the same block.
- Very frequent small events (each dice roll as a separate request).
- Anything where a trusted operator is acceptable and speed matters: a plain service is simpler and cheaper there.
- Ordinary payments and transfers: they do not need randomness.

## 4. How to use it economically
- One request gives 32 bytes. From one number you can derive as many values as you like by hashing it with an index: `hash(number, 0)`, `hash(number, 1)` and so on. There is no need to request each one.
- Request a number once per event (a raffle, a round, a launch), not per action.
- The cost of a request is the reward (from 0.01 USDC) plus the gas of the call. The fulfillment is paid for by the relayer out of the reward.

## 5. How to connect (three steps)
1. Inherit the `RandomnessConsumer` base and set the Hub address.
2. Freeze the inputs and call the request with a reward.
3. Receive the number in `_onRandomness`, store it and finish the event with a separate action. Provide your own timeout.

The current Hub on Arc Testnet: `0xe754e552fae5ba55726f0e97a196ee9e19e2f9d2`.

## 6. Comparison with alternatives (based on public descriptions)

| | Orion (RandomnessHub) | D20DAO on Arc | Chainlink VRF | Pyth Entropy | Block hash |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| The owner can change prices or rules | No | Yes, by their description | Set by the oracle network | Set by the provider | No |
| The result cannot be chosen | Yes if drand is honest | Yes if the sources are honest | Yes, but an operator can refuse to answer | Depends on the provider | No, the block producer influences it |
| Presence on Arc | Yes (this protocol) | Yes | Not found in the Arc announcement | Not found | Yes |
| Cost | Reward from 0.01 USDC plus gas | Minimum 0.02 USDC | Subscription and token | Protocol fee | Free |
| Independent audit | Not done | I did not check | Yes | Yes | Not applicable |
| Maturity | New, on a test network | A young project | Mature | Mature | Not applicable |

The main practical caveat: Chainlink and Pyth are more mature and proven by time. Orion wins by having no owner and by payment in USDC on Arc, but loses on maturity and audits.
